meta {
  name: LOCATION UPDATE - Local (Mock)
}

docs {
  Local end-to-end test of the User Location Update Workflow WITHOUT the bot-wrapper service,
  using the pluggable Mock messaging provider.

  Prerequisites
  -------------
  1. Run workflow-service locally (BASE_URL = http://localhost:2345/api/v1).
  2. The "User Location Update Workflow - V1" schema must already be constructed/seeded in the
     local DB. SCHEMA_ID, TENANT_ID, API_KEY, USER_PHONE, MODERATOR_PASSWORD must be set in the
     environment.
  3. Run the service with the Mock messaging provider active — set in the config file the service
     loads:  Communication.Messaging.Provider = "Mock"  (service.config.local.json is already "Mock",
     so NODE_ENV=local works out of the box; otherwise set it in service.config.json). The endpoint
     /api/v1/debug/messages is ALWAYS available, but it only captures/returns data under the Mock
     provider (BotWrapper delivers instead, leaving the store empty). Messages keep their real
     channel, e.g. "WhatsApp". Restart the service after changing the config.

  How verification works
  ----------------------
  The Mock provider captures each outbound message in memory (instead of POSTing to bot-wrapper).
  We read them from GET /api/v1/debug/messages. Each captured message carries its SchemaInstanceId,
  so step 04 derives the exact instance id from the captured prompt — this avoids the schema-instance
  search, which orders by CreatedAt and cannot reliably return the instance you just started.

  IMPORTANT — use a fresh phone per run
  -------------------------------------
  When you send "Hi" without a SchemaInstanceId, the engine RESUMES any existing non-terminated
  instance for that phone (e.g. one parked waiting for a location) instead of starting fresh — a
  resumed instance often produces no new message. To test a clean run, change USER_PHONE to a new
  value each time, or finish/terminate the previous instance first.

  Note on external calls
  ----------------------
  The registration check (EN_CHECK -> GET /responders/exists) and the update (EN2 -> PUT /responders)
  are RestApiCall actions to the external location service; they are NOT mocked. If the phone isn't
  a registered responder (or the endpoint is unreachable) the workflow goes down the not-registered
  path and terminates before the location prompt/confirmation.

  Run order
  ---------
  01 login -> 02 clear captured -> 03 start ("Hi") -> 04 resolve instance id (from captured msg)
  -> 05 captured (prompt) -> 06 send location -> 07 captured (confirmation) -> 08 activity history.
}
